```shiploop-state
{
  "evidence_refs": ["S1", "S2"],
  "headline": "Intake established a small Python seat-reservation service and its test boundaries, with defaults for unspecified behavior.",
  "outcome": "done",
  "answers": [
    {
      "id": "K1.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Organisers and people use a public HTTP JSON API; the runtime does not identify individual users."
    },
    {
      "id": "K1.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "An HTTP client can create an event, reserve its seats one at a time, and observe rejection when no seats remain; tests demonstrate this at the HTTP boundary."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.1",
      "basis": "S1",
      "answer": "In scope: a small Python HTTP service for creating events with seat capacity and reserving seats, plus tests. Out of scope: additional product features and implementation during intake."
    },
    {
      "id": "K2.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The repository is empty, so no existing application behavior is identified for preservation."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume POST /events accepts JSON {\"seats\": positive integer} and returns 201 with {\"id\": string, \"seats\": integer, \"remaining\": integer}. Assume POST /events/{id}/reservations accepts an empty JSON object, reserves one seat, and returns 201 with {\"id\": string, \"event_id\": string, \"remaining\": integer}. IDs are opaque strings; errors have shape {\"error\": string}."
    },
    {
      "id": "K3.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Both operations return 400 for malformed or invalid input and 415 for a non-JSON content type. Reservation returns 404 for an unknown event and 409 when sold out. Unexpected server failures return 500 without exposing internal details."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Capacity is a positive integer; each successful reservation consumes exactly one seat, remaining seats never fall below zero, and reservations stop succeeding when the event is sold out."
    },
    {
      "id": "K4.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Reject malformed JSON, missing or extra fields, non-integer seat counts including booleans, non-positive seat counts, and reservation bodies other than an empty object."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The service owns event capacity, remaining capacity, and reservation identifiers in process memory. State is shared by requests to that process and lasts until it exits or restarts."
    },
    {
      "id": "K5.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Anyone who can reach the API can submit its requests and see their responses; no user-specific privacy is assumed. Concurrent reservations must update capacity atomically, and a restart clears all state."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.1",
      "basis": "inspect",
      "answer": "Discovery should establish the available Python runtime; assume Python 3.11 or later if the environment offers no guidance."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Use the Python standard library for the service and tests; assume no third-party dependencies."
    },
    {
      "id": "K6.3",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Assume a configurable listen address and port, defaulting to 127.0.0.1:8000, with no required environment variables or configuration files."
    },
    {
      "id": "K7.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Keep HTTP request handling separate from the in-memory event and reservation operations so domain behavior and HTTP behavior can be tested independently."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Use standard-library unittest, run with python -m unittest discover."
    },
    {
      "id": "K8.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Exercise the real HTTP boundary for event creation, reservation, invalid input, unknown events, sold-out events, and concurrent attempts for the last seat; verify response status and body and that successful reservations do not exceed capacity."
    },
    {
      "id": "K9.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "Deliver the service and tests in the run's source checkout; assume no deployed target or external access is required. The checkout's outcome becomes visible on final return."
    },
    {
      "id": "K10.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "No numerical performance or interface-polish targets are specified. Assume a small local HTTP service with no authentication, no browser interface, and basic input validation; this rules out private or production-grade deployment guarantees."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.1",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "The main risks are overselling under concurrent requests and a mismatch between assumed API, persistence, or access behavior and the intended use."
    },
    {
      "id": "K11.2",
      "basis": "default",
      "answer": "An HTTP concurrency test exposes overselling early; discovery of runtime and delivery constraints can expose incompatible assumptions before implementation."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "S1 [fact] Outcome: organisers can create capacity-limited events and people can reserve seats through a small Python HTTP service; [assumption] deliver a public JSON API in the source checkout, with one seat per reservation, atomic capacity updates, and process-local state that is shared across requests and lost on restart; the service does not identify actors, distinguishes no private data by user, and assumes only the Python standard library provides runtime capabilities; [boundary] the repository is empty, this unattended intake implements nothing, no prior run is identified, and no external release is authorized or required; [risk] concurrent overselling or an unsuitable API, persistence, or access default could cause rejection; [open question] discovery must first inspect the available environment for the Python runtime and delivery constraints, which blocks confirming runtime compatibility and how the service can be run; no team, channel, internal site, document, private repository, owner, or ambiguous domain term is named as a source hint, and any sensitive locator discovered belongs only in an authorized private store, never in committed KNOWLEDGE/; [visibility] the isolated source checkout remains at its snapshot until final return, and any separately authorized deployed outcome exists only after release. S2 [fact] Outcome: include tests; [assumption] standard-library tests exercise the HTTP boundary and concurrent capacity limit, and become usable with the delivered checkout at final return."
}
```
